# /etc/h713/warp.conf -- the keystone of h713-warp.
#
# Eight inward offsets in per-mille of the panel, 0..1000. 0 = the corner is
# untouched, larger pulls that corner towards the centre of the picture. The
# names are the PHYSICAL corners as the picture stands on the wall:
#
#     tl ------- tr          tl = top left      tr = top right
#      |         |           bl = bottom left   br = bottom right
#     bl ------- br
#
# x of a corner is limited by the corner at the other end of its row (tl by
# tr, bl by br), y by the one at the other end of its column (tl by bl, tr by
# br), so the picture can never be pulled past its own width or height. That
# is the vendor's own clamp, KeystoneUtils.setkeystoneValue.
#
# The file is also what "h713-warp ctl keystone set|nudge|reset" writes, whole
# and atomically, so a keystone set with the remote survives a reboot. Editing
# it by hand is allowed; a value that is not a number 0..1000 is dropped with
# its line number in the journal.
#
# ALL EIGHT AT 0 IS THE IDENTITY: the daemon does not open the render node at
# all, h713-tv shows the capture ring exactly as it does without this service,
# and nothing costs latency or memory. That is the state a fresh image is in.

# tl is the wall's TOP LEFT as the audience sees it, tr top right, bl bottom left,
# br bottom right - verified on the HY310 on 22.09.2026 (plan/keystone T21: tl pulled
# in by 150 per-mille moved the top left corner of the projected picture).
tl_x  = 0
tl_y  = 0
tr_x  = 0
tr_y  = 0
bl_x  = 0
bl_y  = 0
br_x  = 0
br_y  = 0

# The state to come up in. "on" with all eight values 0 is the bypass above,
# so it is safe; the warp only starts the GPU when a corner is not 0.
# "h713-warp ctl on|off" changes the running state and does NOT change this
# line -- this is what the next start does.
warp  = off
